WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.455
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:09.141
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.441 --> 00:00:15.437
Unit fourteen: Change Management. Today you will propose tactful solutions with could + suggestion.

4
00:00:15.737 --> 00:00:18.401
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:22.507
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:24.607
Change management.

7
00:00:25.617 --> 00:00:29.938
Change management means the structured approach to transitioning people through change.

8
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:33.629
Change management was budgeted from the start of the merger.

9
00:00:34.217 --> 00:00:37.299
Your turn. Say: Change management.

10
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:41.901
Change management.

11
00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.174
Resistance to change.

12
00:00:45.183 --> 00:00:48.576
Resistance to change means opposition to new ways of working.

13
00:00:49.183 --> 00:00:52.924
Resistance to change often stems from fear of losing competence.

14
00:00:53.517 --> 00:00:56.831
Your turn. Say: Resistance to change.

15
00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:01.283
Resistance to change.

16
00:01:02.017 --> 00:01:03.006
Buy-in.

17
00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:07.375
Buy-in means genuine agreement and commitment from those affected.

18
00:01:07.983 --> 00:01:10.552
Without buy-in from teachers, the reform stalled.

19
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:13.477
Your turn. Say: Buy-in.

20
00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:17.427
Buy-in.

21
00:01:18.183 --> 00:01:19.533
Transition plan.

22
00:01:20.517 --> 00:01:24.710
Transition plan means a schedule for moving from the old system to the new.

23
00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:28.791
The transition plan allowed six months of parallel systems.

24
00:01:29.383 --> 00:01:32.349
Your turn. Say: Transition plan.

25
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.607
Transition plan.

26
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:38.955
Communication strategy.

27
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:44.852
Communication strategy means a planned approach to informing and engaging people.

28
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.785
A two-way communication strategy reduced rumours.

29
00:01:49.383 --> 00:01:52.210
Your turn. Say: Communication strategy.

30
00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:56.962
Communication strategy.

31
00:01:57.717 --> 00:01:58.787
Adoption.

32
00:01:59.783 --> 00:02:03.060
Adoption means the uptake and regular use of something new.

33
00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.741
Adoption of the app reached 80% within a year.

34
00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:10.304
Your turn. Say: Adoption.

35
00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:14.179
Adoption.

36
00:02:14.917 --> 00:02:16.266
Sustainability.

37
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:21.792
Sustainability means the capacity of a change to last over time.

38
00:02:22.383 --> 00:02:25.405
Sustainability depends on embedding new habits.

39
00:02:26.017 --> 00:02:28.878
Your turn. Say: Sustainability.

40
00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:33.060
Sustainability.

41
00:02:33.817 --> 00:02:34.736
Momentum.

42
00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:39.329
Momentum means the driving force gained as progress accumulates.

43
00:02:39.917 --> 00:02:42.938
Early successes built momentum for wider reform.

44
00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:46.005
Your turn. Say: Momentum.

45
00:02:48.867 --> 00:02:49.786
Momentum.

46
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.471
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.317 --> 00:03:02.183
Visal, the ministry is introducing digital attendance, but many older teachers are refusing to use it. What could help?

48
00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:11.996
A clear communication strategy could reduce resistance. Teachers need to understand why the change matters, not just how to use the app.

49
00:03:13.083 --> 00:03:14.560
Wouldn't training be enough?

50
00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:26.882
Training helps, but fear of looking incompetent is often the real barrier. The ministry could pair each older teacher with a younger colleague for support.

51
00:03:27.983 --> 00:03:29.739
And if some still refuse?

52
00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:40.770
They could invite respected sceptics to pilot the system first. Once influential colleagues endorse it, momentum usually follows.

53
00:03:41.933 --> 00:03:43.515
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:46.651
Grammar focus: Could plus suggestion.

55
00:03:46.951 --> 00:03:48.220
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:48.917 --> 00:03:52.251
A clear communication strategy could reduce resistance.

57
00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:56.044
The ministry could pair each older teacher with a mentor.

58
00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:59.751
They could perhaps invite sceptics to pilot the system.

59
00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:12.466
'Could + base verb' offers suggestions tactfully, leaving the decision to the listener — far more diplomatic than 'must' or 'should' in consulting. Strengthen with 'could perhaps', 'might also', or 'one option could be to…'.

60
00:04:13.217 --> 00:04:17.657
Be careful! Don't say: A strategy could reduces resistance.

61
00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.377
Say: A strategy could reduce resistance.

62
00:04:22.677 --> 00:04:24.131
Could + base verb.

63
00:04:24.933 --> 00:04:27.690
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.047
Why Change Fails.

65
00:04:30.347 --> 00:04:38.743
Studies frequently claim that around seventy per cent of organisational change initiatives fail to achieve their goals, although the precise figure is debated.

66
00:04:39.733 --> 00:04:46.841
Failure rarely results from poor technical design; instead, it usually reflects insufficient attention to people's concerns.

67
00:04:47.833 --> 00:04:55.649
John Kotter's model emphasises creating urgency, building a guiding coalition and generating short-term wins that could sustain momentum.

68
00:04:56.633 --> 00:05:03.556
Resistance should not be dismissed as stubbornness, since it often contains valuable information about practical obstacles.

69
00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:11.175
Leaders could therefore treat resisters as consultants, transforming opposition into ownership and greatly improving adoption.

70
00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:16.315
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:19.533
A short pilot blank build confidence.

72
00:05:19.833 --> 00:05:23.312
A, could. B, could to. C, could be.

73
00:05:26.867 --> 00:05:28.495
The answer is A: could.

74
00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:32.776
Genuine agreement and commitment from those affected is blank.

75
00:05:33.076 --> 00:05:36.462
A, buy-in. B, adoption. C, momentum.

76
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.895
The answer is A: buy-in.

77
00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:46.234
The driving force gained as progress accumulates is blank.

78
00:05:46.534 --> 00:05:50.814
A, resistance. B, transition plan. C, momentum.

79
00:05:54.367 --> 00:05:56.784
The answer is C: momentum.

80
00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:00.251
Choose the most diplomatic suggestion.

81
00:06:00.551 --> 00:06:07.572
A, You must retrain everyone now.. B, You could perhaps start with a small pilot.. C, Retrain everyone or fail..

82
00:06:11.133 --> 00:06:14.747
The answer is B: You could perhaps start with a small pilot..

83
00:06:15.883 --> 00:06:18.823
According to the reading, resistance often contains…

84
00:06:19.123 --> 00:06:25.331
A, valuable information about obstacles. B, nothing useful. C, only stubbornness.

85
00:06:28.867 --> 00:06:32.538
The answer is A: valuable information about obstacles.

86
00:06:33.733 --> 00:06:36.862
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:37.717 --> 00:06:41.597
What strategy could help reduce resistance to organizational change?

88
00:06:58.383 --> 00:06:59.860
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:34.435
One effective strategy could be to involve employees in designing the change rather than simply announcing it. When people help shape a new system, they understand its purpose and feel a sense of ownership. Leaders could also identify respected colleagues to act as early adopters, since peers are often more persuasive than managers. For example, a school introducing digital grading could let experienced teachers test it first. Their endorsement could build momentum faster than any directive.

90
00:07:34.735 --> 00:07:36.363
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:41.203
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and could plus suggestion.

92
00:07:41.503 --> 00:07:46.465
Today's value is adaptability. Be ready for rain or sun and change your plans.

93
00:07:46.765 --> 00:07:49.487
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:49.787 --> 00:07:52.591
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

